1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,560
From Monday to Friday, many of us have an early start and a long day.
周一到周五，我们很多人都早起晚归。

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:14,930
By the time we've gone to bed and managed to fall asleep, we've been woken up by the alarm to do it all again.
等到好不容易上床入睡，闹钟又会把我们叫醒，开启新的一天。

3
00:00:14,930 --> 00:00:19,270
Come the weekend, and we're totally exhausted.
到了周末，我们早已筋疲力尽。

4
00:00:19,270 --> 00:00:27,700
We sleep in way past our usual wake-up time just to stay in sync enough to start again on Monday.
为了能在周一重新振作，我们会睡到大中午，远远超过平时的起床时间。

5
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:30,550
Welcome to social jet lag.
这就是“社交时差”。

6
00:00:30,550 --> 00:00:42,960
That's the term for the disparity between our working-week sleeping pattern, when our sleep times relate to our responsibilities, and the weekend, when we can wake when we choose.
这个术语指的是工作日和周末睡眠模式的差异：工作日的睡眠时间要配合工作和责任，而周末我们可以随心所欲地起床。

7
00:00:42,960 --> 00:00:49,940
And depending on what type of person you are, the difference can be significant.
根据个人类型不同，这种差异可能会非常明显。

8
00:00:49,940 --> 00:01:04,360
For night owls - those whose natural rhythm is to wake and go to bed later - there can be significant health-related issues, according to a recent study published by Taylor and Francis Group online.
泰勒&弗朗西斯集团近期在网上发布的一项研究表明，对于夜猫子——那些天生习惯晚睡晚起的人来说，这可能会引发严重的健康问题。

9
00:01:04,360 --> 00:01:18,760
The study concludes the further the divergence between working-week and weekend sleep times, the greater the health issues – including a higher risk of heart disease and other metabolic problems.
研究得出结论：工作日和周末的睡眠时间差异越大，健康问题就越严重，包括患心脏病和其他代谢疾病的风险更高。

10
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:28,470
And because so many jobs and tasks start early, night owls are effectively forced into harmonising with the early birds.
而且由于很多工作和任务都需要早起，夜猫子们实际上被迫要和早起的人保持一致的作息。

11
00:01:28,470 --> 00:01:35,000
So what can night owls do: force themselves to integrate by sacrificing their lie in?
那么夜猫子们该怎么办呢？

12
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:47,760
'It's the worst thing you can do' says Professor Till Roenneberg, professor of chronobiology at the Institute of Medical Psychology at Ludwig-Maximilian University in Munich.
难道要牺牲赖床时间，强迫自己融入早起的作息吗？

13
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:52,960
This is because people's sleep pattern is half determined by genetics.
慕尼黑路德维希-马克西米利安大学医学心理学研究所的时间生物学教授蒂尔·伦内伯格说：“这是最糟糕的做法。”

14
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:56,860
The other half correlates with their age and environment.
因为人的睡眠模式一半由基因决定，另一半与年龄和环境相关。

15
00:01:56,860 --> 00:02:02,880
Getting less sleep is unlikely to realign your genetic tendencies.
减少睡眠并不能改变你的基因倾向。

16
00:02:02,880 --> 00:02:08,130
Our bodies evolved to coordinate with the rise and fall of the Sun.
我们的身体进化得与太阳的升落保持同步。

17
00:02:08,130 --> 00:02:11,810
We should feel sleepy as the light dissipates.
当光线逐渐消失时，我们就会感到困倦。

18
00:02:11,810 --> 00:02:20,930
But modern life, with its artificial light and modern devices, such as computers and smartphones, means we have deviated.
但现代生活中的人造光源和电脑、智能手机等电子设备，让我们偏离了这种自然节律。

19
00:02:20,930 --> 00:02:28,540
Now we are exposed to more light for longer periods of time, keeping our bodies awake longer.
现在我们暴露在光线中的时间更长，这让身体保持清醒的时间也随之延长。

20
00:02:28,540 --> 00:02:36,560
For night owls, who already tend to sleep later, this delays things even further.
对于本就倾向于晚睡的夜猫子来说，这会让他们的入睡时间更晚。

21
00:02:36,560 --> 00:02:48,210
One solution, beyond changing society's early-start tendencies, is to reorient our body clock by manipulating our exposure to light.
除了改变社会普遍早起的趋势之外，还有一个解决方案：通过控制光照来调整生物钟。

22
00:02:48,210 --> 00:03:02,910
By taking more sunlight in the morning and minimising the amount of artificial light we are exposed to in the evening – particularly on electronic devices - we can rebalance our bodies to feel sleepy earlier.
早上多晒太阳，晚上尽量减少人造光源的接触——尤其是电子设备发出的光线——这样可以重新平衡身体状态，让我们更早感到困倦。

23
00:03:02,910 --> 00:03:09,440
It's far from easy, but better that than losing your whole weekend to sleep.
这绝非易事，但总比整个周末都用来补觉要好。
